{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"4ef6c5eacfc306507942fbb08b71f7c94ddda46b14182d4f384ab3debfb6c6a5","slug":"pages.m11-introduction-to-normal-random-variables-3-of-3","location":"/pages/153-m11-introduction-to-normal-random-variables-3-of-3.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Standardizing Values: The z-score","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"City College of San Francisco","given":"City College","non_dropping_particle":"of","family":"San Francisco"},"name":"City College of San Francisco","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","CC":true}},"numbering":{"title":{"enabled":true,"offset":0}},"enumerator":"138","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"153-m11-introduction-to-normal-random-variables-3-of-3.md","url":"/textbook/build/153-m11-introduction-7789dc7452ad2fb5fb9ebf0b8da80d13.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let’s go back to our example of foot length:","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"mSeP1hKDjy"}],"key":"Y3YWTKyIyZ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"How likely or unlikely is it for a male’s foot length to be more than 13 inches?","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"eU2SLf1ciK"}],"key":"I7cMcBrnQO"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Since 13 inches doesn’t happen to be exactly 1, 2, or 3 standard deviations away from the mean, we would only be able to give a very rough estimate of the probability at this point. Clearly, the Standard Deviation Rule only describes the tip of the iceberg, and while it serves well as an introduction to the normal curve, and gives us a good sense of what would be considered likely and unlikely values, it is very limited in the probability questions it can help us answer.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"N1GM6Q2X4B"}],"key":"SYWbsrkFsN"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here is another familiar normal distribution: scores on the math portion of the SAT, which are approximately normal with mean μ = 500 and standard deviation σ = 100. Suppose we are interested in knowing the probability that a randomly selected student will score 633 or more. Again, 633 does not fall exactly 1, 2, or 3 standard deviations above the mean. Notice, however, that an SAT score of 633 and a foot length of 13 are both about 1/3 of the way between 1 and 2 standard deviations. As you continue to read this page, you’ll realize that this positioning relative to the mean is the key to finding probabilities.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"hOi2ljMnDp"}],"key":"JnKO3qx87t"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Finding Probabilities for a Normal Random Variable","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"fjKWHndtBk"}],"identifier":"finding-probabilities-for-a-normal-random-variable","label":"Finding Probabilities for a Normal Random Variable","html_id":"finding-probabilities-for-a-normal-random-variable","implicit":true,"key":"IjOneDwCmq"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As we saw, the Standard Deviation Rule is very limited in helping us answer probability questions, and is basically limited to questions involving values that fall exactly 1, 2, and 3 standard deviations away from the mean. How do we answer probability questions in general? The key is the position of the value relative to the mean, measured in standard deviations.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"YhQ0l6Hacx"}],"key":"CTuVNW1zbx"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We can approach the answering of probability questions two possible ways: a table and technology. In the next sections, you will learn how to use the “standard normal table,” and the same calculations can be done with statistical software or a calculator.","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"UKLhcSUslg"}],"key":"nGUfld1DoH"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Standardizing Values","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"R0bIx2TB27"}],"identifier":"standardizing-values","label":"Standardizing Values","html_id":"standardizing-values","implicit":true,"key":"nj44T7ly5z"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first step to assessing a probability associated with a normal value is to determine the ","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"gUcEjP306t"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"relative","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"dfIpoA1Lgn"}],"key":"qPxFp3PkDE"},{"type":"text","value":" value with respect to all the other values taken by that normal variable. This is accomplished by determining how many standard deviations below or above the mean that value is.","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"CZw9TzSS1D"}],"key":"DxHbDEuAjn"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Foot Length","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"bADDQW38MM"}],"key":"kc1GfuElQJ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"How many standard deviations below or above the mean male foot length is 13 inches? Since the mean is 11 inches, 13 inches is 2 inches above the mean. Since a standard deviation is 1.5 inches, this would be 2 / 1.5 = 1.33 standard deviations above the mean. Combining these two steps, we could write:","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"wKtJDlfncO"}],"key":"AMREMThYgS"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"(13 in. − 11 in.) / (1.5 inches per standard deviation) = (13 − 11) / 1.5 standard deviations = +1.33 standard deviations.","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"VE8TohIqSG"}],"key":"wDErgLGvf3"}],"class":"tip","key":"SFAl3c0ZL5"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the language of statistics, we have just found the ","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"hARjUjRFLw"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"z-score","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"qnHq6s2fKs"}],"key":"kSRlfUQbpi"},{"type":"text","value":" for a male foot length of 13 inches to be z = +1.33. Or, to put it another way, we have ","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"jdgVDDNvly"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"standardized","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"uIJpIaKTQV"}],"key":"NpjmeKtpVx"},{"type":"text","value":" the value of 13. In general, the standardized value z tells how many standard deviations below or above the mean the original value is, and is calculated as follows:","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"XygyEpU74h"}],"key":"yCvt9RM0kG"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"z-score = (value − mean)/standard deviation","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"XPo0DKS0m2"}],"key":"dgpDq7kf61"}],"key":"snZGh6m0YW"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The convention is to denote a value of our normal random variable X with the letter “x.” Since the mean is written ","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"EEkvtI1j7A"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\mu","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">μ</span></span></span></span>","key":"ZuPx5cKqNt"},{"type":"text","value":" and the standard deviation ","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"y01Khk7W6m"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\sigma","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">σ</span></span></span></span>","key":"MhEJ6etWqT"},{"type":"text","value":", we may write the standardized value as","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"wo0wsG5xP2"}],"key":"E9zOBkCspT"},{"type":"math","value":"z=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mi>σ</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.04398em;\">z</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.9463em;vertical-align:-0.686em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span